--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
what the lines show
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

* The altar at (17,17) is the ONLY altar on the map. It is locked by the
  enemy. Before F6 the banker had no bank candidate at all and the
  economy went inert on this map.
* "lockbreak+5" on the contest-flag line is the PRICED break cost: the
  candidate's score carried the engine constant ALTAR_LOCK_HITS (5) on top
  of the walk (d) and the tribute channel (exp=90). The lock did not gate
  the candidate out — it made it more expensive, and the argmax still
  chose it because there was nothing else to choose.
* 10 "f6: lockbreak hit" lines in total, in two rounds of 5 (r=547..552
  and r=721..725). Five hits per lock is exactly ALTAR_LOCK_HITS: the
  engine converts the tribute attempt into an attack on the lock, the
  lock's hits_remaining goes 5 -> 4 -> 3 -> 2 -> 1 -> gone, and on the
  sixth attempt the submit becomes a real tribute.
* The mid-sequence line "s22: bank contest flag: d=5 g=61 contest
  altar=(17,17)" (no "lockbreak+5") is the turn the lock was already
  gone: the candidate became an ordinary open-altar bank again, at the
  same altar.
* The per-champion numbering (#1, #2, #3) is the per-champion counter:
  each champion runs in its own process with its own Bot instance, so
  two champions each landed several of the five hits.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
why there is no separate "attack the lock" action
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Probed directly against the engine (the /tmp/opencode/f6probe/attacker
probe, replay9): me.submit_gems(altar) against an ENEMY-locked altar
returns OK and is auto-converted into a lock attack — hits_remaining went
5 -> 4 -> 3 -> 2 -> 1 -> gone over five attempts and is_submitting_gems()
stayed False the whole time (no tribute channel was started). A
following me.attack(lock) raised Error(41, 'Attack already used.'),
because the conversion had already consumed that turn's attack.

So the action path is: submit with the enemy-lock guard bypassed
(execution.submit_gems_to_altar(..., break_lock=True)). The engine does
the conversion. No new action was added to the bot.
